# Line endings are a property of this repository, not of whoever cloned it.
#
# Git for Windows ships core.autocrlf=true in its system config, so a checkout
# there rewrote every text file to CRLF while the stored blobs stayed LF. The
# working tree and the repository disagreed, and a checker that split on "\n"
# read every rule as having no frontmatter: in JavaScript "." does not match
# "\r", so `(.*)$` fails on a CRLF line. The check went red for a reason that
# had nothing to do with what it measures.
#
# `text=auto` normalises to LF on commit, which was already happening.
# `eol=lf` is the half that was missing: it checks out LF on every platform,
# overriding whatever core.autocrlf the machine was configured with. Being
# committed, it applies to every clone without anyone setting anything.
* text=auto eol=lf

# Shell scripts break outright with CRLF, so say it explicitly rather than
# relying on the wildcard above surviving a future edit.
*.sh text eol=lf

# Detected as binary by content anyway; declared so that a future addition of
# one of these cannot be corrupted by a line-ending rewrite.
*.png binary
*.jpg binary
*.jpeg binary
*.gif binary
*.webp binary
*.ico binary
*.pdf binary
*.zip binary
*.gz binary
*.woff binary
*.woff2 binary
